// expect-error: proof obligation(s) could not be discharged
// FORGE Bad Demo 22 — Signed multiply overflow under #[checked] (correctly rejected)
//
// This program SHOULD FAIL to compile.
//
// `a * b` on unbounded i32 operands can overflow the signed range
// [INT_MIN, INT_MAX]. Under #[checked], Forge emits a sign-split no-overflow
// obligation (CERT INT32-C) and discharges it in bitvector mode. With no bound
// on a and b it cannot be proven — e.g. INT_MAX * 2 overflows.
//
// Notably, the check is not fooled by the INT_MIN * -1 corner: that case is
// also correctly rejected (a naive (a*b)/b == a check would wrongly accept it).
//
// Expected output:
//   ✗  cannot prove 'mul' won't overflow
//   compilation stopped — fix proof failures before proceeding
//
// Fix: bound the operands (e.g. requires -30000 <= a <= 30000, likewise b),
// or drop #[checked] if wraparound is intended. See SOUNDNESS.md section 2.

#[checked]
fn signed_mul(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32
{
    a * b
}
